!deck: Math Identities
!tags: Math Trigonometry Hyperbolic
!type: Basic Math

!id: b1KIhb3ucQ
!front: Why does \( t \mapsto (\cosh t,\sinh t) \) parametrize the right branch of the unit hyperbola?
!back:
For every \( t \in \mathbb{R} \),
\[ \cosh^2 t - \sinh^2 t = 1, \]
and \( \cosh t>0 \), so \( (\cosh t,\sinh t) \) lies on the right branch of \( x^2-y^2=1 \).

![The right branch of the unit hyperbola x^2-y^2=1, traced by t -> (cosh t, sinh t); a sample point at parameter t0 has dashed guides down to its coordinates cosh t0 and sinh t0, with the asymptotes y=x and y=-x shown dashed](img/unit-hyperbola-parametrization.svg)
~~~

!id: nClEzdC7Va
!front: What does \( t \) represent for the point \( (\cosh t,\sinh t) \) on the unit hyperbola?
!back:
It is the hyperbolic angle: twice the signed area of the hyperbolic sector from \( (1,0) \) to \( (\cosh t,\sinh t) \) on \( x^2-y^2=1 \).

Using \( x=\cosh u \), \( y=\sinh u \), the signed sector area is
\[ \frac{1}{2}\int_0^t (x\,dy-y\,dx) = \frac{1}{2}\int_0^t (\cosh^2 u-\sinh^2 u)\,du = \frac{t}{2}. \]

![The hyperbolic sector swept from (1,0) to (cosh t0, sinh t0) on x^2-y^2=1, shaded, bounded by the two straight radii from the origin and the hyperbola arc between them; its area is t0/2](img/hyperbolic-sector.svg)
~~~

!id: cxorTaGaCe
!front: Give a coordinate change that takes \( x^2-y^2=1 \) to \( uv=1 \), and explain why it works.
!back:
Set \[ u=x+y,\qquad v=x-y. \]

Then \[ uv=(x+y)(x-y)=x^2-y^2, \]

so \( x^2-y^2=1 \) becomes \( uv=1 \).

![The right branch of x^2-y^2=1 in the x,y-plane, with the u-axis and v-axis directions overlaid as dashed lines along the asymptotes y=x and y=-x, labeled with the map u=x+y, v=x-y](img/hyperbola-uv-axes.svg)
~~~

!id: BcRlYH18B4
!front: Under \( u=x+y,\ v=x-y \), what happens to the asymptotes \( y=\pm x \) of \( x^2-y^2=1 \)?
!back:
They become the coordinate axes: \( y=x \) gives \( v=0 \) (the \( u \)-axis), and \( y=-x \) gives \( u=0 \) (the \( v \)-axis).

![The right branch of x^2-y^2=1 with the u-axis along y=x and the v-axis along y=-x under u=x+y, v=x-y](img/hyperbola-uv-axes.svg)
~~~

!id: g5nqBsx0sL
!front: Under \( u=x+y,\ v=x-y \), what are the new coordinates of the point \( (\cosh t,\sinh t) \) on \( x^2-y^2=1 \)?
!back:
\[
\begin{align*}
    u &= \cosh t+\sinh t=e^t, \\
    v &= \cosh t-\sinh t=e^{-t}.
\end{align*}
\]

Thus the right branch becomes \[ (u,v)=(e^t,e^{-t}), \] on \( uv=1 \).

![Side by side: the point (cosh t, sinh t) on x^2-y^2=1 on the left, and its image (e^t,e^{-t}) on uv=1 on the right, connected by an arrow labeled u=x+y, v=x-y](img/hyperbola-point-map.svg)
~~~
